    bio: "Gabrielle Émilie Le Tonnelier de Breteuil, Marquise du Châtelet, was born in Paris in 1706 into a family that was aristocratic enough to give her access to the best tutors in France, and unusual enough to let her use them seriously. At a time when mathematics and natural philosophy were considered entirely unsuitable for women, Émilie du Châtelet taught herself Latin, Italian, Greek, and enough advanced mathematics to engage with the foremost scientists of her age on equal terms. By her twenties she was conducting experiments in her own laboratory and corresponding with the leading figures of the European Enlightenment.\n\nHer most celebrated achievement was her French translation of Isaac Newton\'s Principia Mathematica — still the standard French edition of that foundational work, and the only one that has ever been made. She did not merely translate it; she annotated and extended it, adding a commentary that incorporated the latest developments in continental mathematics, including the work of Leibniz on calculus. Her translation made Newton\'s ideas accessible to the French scientific community in a way that the original Latin had not, and her commentary corrected and expanded on the master\'s work in ways that Newton himself had not anticipated.\n\nDu Châtelet also made original scientific contributions of her own. Building on experiments by Willem 's Gravesande, she defended and extended the concept of vis viva — what we now call kinetic energy — arguing against the Newtonian position that a body\'s energy was proportional to its velocity, and demonstrating instead that it was proportional to the square of its velocity. This was a significant and contested finding, and she published it in her Institutions de Physique in 1740. The idea, in refined form, became a cornerstone of classical mechanics.\n\nShe died in September 1749, days after giving birth to her fourth child, at the age of forty-two. Voltaire, her companion and intellectual equal for fifteen years, was devastated. 'I have not lost a mistress,' he wrote, 'I have lost half of myself — a great man who had no fault but that of being a woman.' The compliment was as backhanded as it was sincere, but the underlying point was not wrong: in a world that had decided, before she was born, that she could not be a scientist, Émilie du Châtelet had simply become one anyway."
